Describe this system's peer credentials, so EXTERNAL authentication works

gcredentialsprivate.h is a list of systems and how each one passes the
identity of a socket's peer.  A system not on the list gets no
G_CREDENTIALS_SUPPORTED, and GCredentials is then an object that holds
nothing: g_credentials_get_unix_user() fails and returns -1.

Nothing warns about this.  What happens instead is that the EXTERNAL D-Bus
mechanism -- which authenticates by telling the server which uid you are --
sends (uid_t)-1, the server compares that against the uid it read from the
socket, they differ, and the connection is rejected.  From the far end it
looks like this:

  Failed to initialize Xfconf: Exhausted all available authentication
  mechanisms (tried: EXTERNAL) (available: EXTERNAL)

so xfce4-terminal starts with compiled-in defaults and forgets every
preference it is given.

LikeOS gets an arm of its own rather than being folded into the Linux one,
because it has SO_PEERCRED and struct ucred but NOT SCM_CREDENTIALS.  The two
omitted defines are deliberate and are commented where they are omitted.

--- a/gio/gcredentialsprivate.h
+++ b/gio/gcredentialsprivate.h
@@ -114,6 +114,34 @@
 #define G_CREDENTIALS_SPOOFING_SUPPORTED 1
 #define G_CREDENTIALS_HAS_PID 1
 
+#elif defined(__LIKEOS__)
+/* LikeOS: the same struct ucred and the same SO_PEERCRED as above, and
+ * neither of the two things the Linux arm has beyond them.
+ *
+ * SO_PEERCRED is what actually matters.  Without an arm here GCredentials is
+ * inert -- g_credentials_get_unix_user() fails -- and the EXTERNAL D-Bus
+ * mechanism then authenticates as uid (uid_t)-1, which every bus rejects.
+ * That is not a subtle failure: it is the whole of "Exhausted all available
+ * authentication mechanisms (tried: EXTERNAL)".
+ *
+ * NOT G_CREDENTIALS_UNIX_CREDENTIALS_MESSAGE_SUPPORTED: this system has no
+ * SCM_CREDENTIALS ancillary message.  Nothing is lost by leaving it off --
+ * g_unix_connection_send_credentials() then sends a plain NUL byte and the
+ * receiver reads the credentials with getsockopt(SO_PEERCRED), which is what
+ * dbus itself prefers in any case ("this is much less fragile", says its own
+ * comment beside the call).
+ *
+ * NOT G_CREDENTIALS_SPOOFING_SUPPORTED: the kernel records these at connect
+ * time from the calling process and offers no way to claim to be somebody
+ * else, which is the point of the mechanism.
+ */
+#define G_CREDENTIALS_SUPPORTED 1
+#define G_CREDENTIALS_USE_LINUX_UCRED 1
+#define G_CREDENTIALS_NATIVE_TYPE G_CREDENTIALS_TYPE_LINUX_UCRED
+#define G_CREDENTIALS_NATIVE_SIZE (sizeof (struct ucred))
+#define G_CREDENTIALS_SOCKET_GET_CREDENTIALS_SUPPORTED 1
+#define G_CREDENTIALS_HAS_PID 1
+
 #elif defined(__FreeBSD__)                                  || \
       defined(__FreeBSD_kernel__) /* Debian GNU/kFreeBSD */ || \
       defined(__GNU__)            /* GNU Hurd */            || \
